%% caption: Where your stdout actually goes
sequenceDiagram
    autonumber
    participant P as Process
    participant J as journald
    participant D as Disk journal
    participant F as rsyslog / shipper
    P->>J: writes to stdout and stderr
    Note over P,J: systemd wires both to the journal socket, not to a file
    J->>J: attach metadata — unit, PID, UID, boot id
    J->>D: append, subject to SystemMaxUse and rate limits
    alt rate limit hit
        J-->>P: messages dropped, and it logs that it dropped them
    end
    J->>F: forward for central collection
    Note over D: volatile in /run unless /var/log/journal exists — logs vanish on reboot
